larly active in mastocytoma cells, the source from which $\mathrm{LT}\dot{\mathrm{C}}_{4}$ was first isolated [4]. Most actions of LTs are exerted from the extracellular space by interaction with specific receptors [5]. Therefore, export of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ from a LT-generating cell is a decisive step. The release of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ has been studied in cultured human eosinophils incubated with exogenous $\mathrm{LTA}_{4}$ and characterized as a temperature-sensitive and saturable transport process, which was influenced by exogenous glutathione conjugates and probenecid, a commonly used inhibitor of transport systems for organic anions [6, 7]. Using isolated membrane vesicles from mastocytoma cells, we demonstrated the existence of an ATP-dependent export system involved in the release of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ [8, 9]. This primaryactive transport showed similar characteristics to the ATPdependent leukotriene export carrier present in the canalicular membrane of the liver which transports $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ with the highest affinity [1O], in addition to a number of structurally related amphiphilic anions including glutathione S-conjugates $\scriptstyle[9-13]$ . This transport system is deficient in a mutant rat strain $(\mathrm{T}\mathbf{R}^{-})$ lacking the capacity to secrete leukotrienes from the hepatocyte into bile [10, 14]. ATP-dependent transport of glutathione S-conjugates was originally discovered in inside-out membrane vesicles from human erythrocytes [15]. Primary-active export of exogenous substances from cels is exemplified by the mdr1 gene product $\mathrm{\bfP}$ -glycoprotein $\mathrm{(gp170)}$ , a well characterized export pump that mediates the efflux of certain anti-cancer drugs which are hydrophobic and mostly cationic [16-18]. By contrast, information is lacking with respect to the molecular structure of the ATPdependent transport system for $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ and related conjugates.
In the present study, the ATP-dependent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ transport has been further characterized using membrane vesicles from murine mastocytoma cells. The inhibition by the cyclic peptide cyclosporin A (CsA) and its non-immunosuppressive analog PSC 833 [19, 20] is compared with the effect of probenecid [21] and the quinoline-based $\mathrm{LTD}_{4}$ receptor antagonist MK 571 [22]. CsA and PSC 833 were shown to reverse Pglycoprotein-mediated [19, 20, 23] as well as P-glycoproteinindependent, p-19O-mediated [24] multidrug resistance in tumor cells, and to interfere with the distinct ATP-dependent transport systems in the canalicular membrane of the liver [25]. Direct photoaffinity labeling of mastocytoma membranes with $\mathrm{[^{3}H]L T C_{4}}$ in the presence or absence of transport inhibitors served to identify $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding membrane proteins which should include components of the ATP-depen dent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ export system.
