Immunoblotting was performed essentially according to Towbin [40] using a tank blotting system and a chemiluminescent alkaline phosphatase detection system [41].
## RESULTS
## ATP-dependent transport of $\mathbf{LTC_{4}}$ by mastocytoma membrane vesicles
Several inhibitors of $\mathrm{\Gamma[^{3}H]I T C_{4}}$ transport into mastocytoma membrane vesicles were tested and the $\mathbf{IC}_{50}$ values are shown in Fig. 3. As recognized earlier [8], the $\mathrm{LTD}_{4}$ receptor antagonist MK 571 was the most potent transport inhibitor with half-maximal inhibition at $1\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The type of inhibition was competitive with respect to $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ with a $K_{\mathrm{i}}$ value of $0.8\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig.2). Probenecid interfered with ATP-dependent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ transport only at relatively high concentrations with an $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ value of $7\ i\ \upmu\mathbf{M}$ (Fig.3). CsA caused half-maximal inhibition at $3\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ，whereas its non-immunosuppressive analog PSC 833 was less effective with half-maximal inhibition at $21~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . Double-reciprocal plots in the absence or presence of ${5\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ CsA or $20\upmu\mathrm{M}$ PSC 833 with $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ concentrations between $10\mathrm{nM}$ and ${500}\mathrm{nM}$ indicated $K_{\mathrm{i}}$ values of $4.5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for CsA and $30\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for PSC 833 with a competitive type of inhibition.
## $\mathbf{LTC_{4}}$ -binding proteins in mastocytoma membranes identified by photoaffinity labeling
Mastocytoma membrane vesicles were photoaffinity labeled with $\mathrm{\lceil^{3}H\rfloor L T C_{4}}$ yielding two predominantly labeled proteins: one with a molecular mass of about $190\mathrm{{kDa}}$ and one of about $35\mathrm{{kDa}}$ (Fig. 4A). In addition there were minor labeled proteins in the molecular mass range of $100\mathrm{kDa}$ (Fig. 4A). In order to separate proteins which are loosely associated with the membrane, we washed the labeled membranes with a buffer containing $2\%$ sodium taurocholate. As shown in Fig. 4B, the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -labeled 35-kDa protein remained in the taurocholate-insoluble pellet, and was separated on a $15\%$ acrylamide gel for better resolution of proteins in this molecular mass range. Labeling with azido[α- $^{32}\mathrm{Pl}$ ATP indicated a predominant labeling in the range of $35\mathrm{kDa}$ (Fig. 4B). Membrane proteins labeled with $[^{35}\mathrm{S}]\mathrm{ATP}[\gamma\mathrm{S}]$ exhibited apparent molecular masses of about $25\mathrm{{kDa}}$ $35\mathrm{kDa}$ $50\up k\mathrm{Da}$ ，and $150\mathrm{kDa}$ (not shown).
The $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -labeled $190\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein also remained in the membrane pellet after taurocholate extraction (Fig. 5). Better separation of membrane proteins with a molecular mass above $100\mathrm{{kDa}}$ was achieved by SDS/PAGE in the presence of $5\mathbf{M}$ urea. As shown in Fig. 5 the $\mathrm{[^{3}H]I T C_{4}}$ labeling of the $190\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein was specifically competed for by the transport inhibitor MK 571 $(50\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . The minor peaks labeled in the range of $100\mathrm{{kDa}}$ were also decreased (Fig.5), whereas the incorporation of radioactivity into the $35\mathrm{-}\mathbf{k}\mathrm{D}\mathbf{a}$ protein was not significantly suppressed by MK 571. Photoaffinity labeling with $\mathrm{[^{3}H]L T C_{4}}$ was also performed in the presence of $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ CsA; however, these competition studies did not allow for a clear interpretation since labeling of many proteins decreased in a non-specific manner.
Treatment of the $[\mathrm{^{3}H]L T C_{4}}$ -labeled membrane proteins with peptide N-glycosidase $\mathrm{~\bf~F~}$ resulted in the appearance of a labeled $140{\cdot}\mathrm{{kDa}}$ polypeptide suggesting that the $190\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein is a glycoprotein (Fig. 6A). After the same procedure, no additional labeled protein was observed after SDS/PAGE on a $15\%$ acrylamide gel used to separate the labeled 35-kDa protein (Fig. 6B).
Further characterization of the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding proteins was performed by two-dimensional electrophoresis (IEF followed by SDS/PAGE) which indicated a mean isoelectric point for the $190\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein at $\mathrm{pH}6.3$ with a range from $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ to $\mathsf{p H}6.7$ and for the $35\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein an isoelectric point at $\mathrm{pH}6.8$ (Fig.7).
Immunoblots using the anti-P-glycoprotein monoclonal antibody C219 revealed only trace amounts of $\mathbf{P}$ -glycoprotein with an apparent molecular mass of $150{-}160\mathrm{kDa}$ indicating that the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding $190\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein was not recognized by this antibody and is distinct from P-glycoprotein.
## DISCUSSION
Release of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ from leukotriene-synthesizing cells is mediated by an ATP-dependent export carrier [8] whereas primary-active ATP-dependent transport is insignificant with $\bar{\mathbf{L}}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{B}_{4}$ as a substrate [9]. Selectjve inhibition of the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ export can be useful for the characterization of the transport system (Figs 2 and 5) and may serve as a novel pharmacological approach to interfere selectively with $\mathrm{LT}\dot{\mathrm{C}}_{4}$ generation under pathophysiological conditions [9]. The release of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ from preloaded cells was shown to be decreased in the pres ence of the organic anion transport inhibitor probenecid [7]. Probenecid also inhibited the ATP-dependent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ transport by mastocytoma membrane vesicles (Fig.3); however, the concentration required was rather high compared to the potent effect of the leukotriene structural analog MK 571 (Figs 2 and 3). The cyclic peptide CsA and its non-immunosuppressive analog PSC 833 are potent drugs to reverse the multidrug resistance phenotype in tumor cells expressing high levels of the mdr1 gene product $\mathbf{P}$ -glycoprotein [23]. PSC 833 differs from CsA only in the structure of two of the 11 amino acids resulting in a loss of immunosuppressive properties [19, 20]. The multidrug-resistance reversing activity of PSC 833 in $\mathbf{P}$ -glycoprotein-overexpressing cell lines was 10-fold higher than that of CsA [19, 20].However, CsA was a much more potent inhibitor of the ATP-dependent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ export from mastocytoma cells than PSC 833 with $K_{\mathrm{i}}$ values of $4.5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $30\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively (Fig. 3). A similar relative potency of CsA and PSC 833 was observed for the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ export carrier in the hepatocyte canalicular membrane, whereas P-glycoprotein-mediated daunorubicin transport was more sensitive to inhibition by PSC 833 than by CsA [25].
The existence of an ATP-dependent transport system for $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ ， other glutathione S-conjugates, and other cysteinyl leukotrienes is well established by kinetic studies in various tissues $\{8^{--13}$ ，15,42, 43]; however, almost nothing was known about the molecular properties of these ATP-dependent $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ or glutathione S-conjugate transporters. To identify $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding proteins in mastocytoma membranes, which should include components of the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ transport system, we applied direct photoaffinity labeling with $^3\mathrm{H}$ -labeled $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ as a photolabile ligand of high affinity. Two proteins were predominantly labeled, a membrane protein of $35\mathrm{kDa}$ and a glycoprotein with an apparent molecular mass of about $190~\mathrm{{kDa}}$ ，which decreased to about $140\mathrm{kDa}$ after peptide N-glycosidase $\mathrm{~F~}$ treatment (Figs $4{-}7;$ . A minor labeling was also observed in the molecular mass range of $100\mathrm{kDa}$ .Azi$\mathrm{do}[\alpha\mathrm{-}^{32}\mathrm{P}]\mathrm{ATP}$ predominantly labeled the $35\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein. A 38-kDa protein with $S.$ -(2,4-dinitrophenyl)glutathione-dependent ATPase activity has been purified from human liver and erythrocytes [44, 45]. However, a direct connection between ATP-dependent glutathione S-conjugate transport and the isolated protein was not demonstrated. Incubation of $\mathrm{LTA}_{4}$ with mastocytoma membrane vesicles in the presence of glutathione resulted in the formation of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ (unpublished results), indicating the presence of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ synthase as another potential $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding protein in our membrane preparation. $\bar{\mathrm{LTC}}_{4}$ synthase was found to be a unique membrane-bound enzyme distinct from other cytosolic and microsomal glutathione $S.$ -transferases [36, 37, 46-48]. Recent studies in dimethylsulfoxide-differentiated U 937 cells indicated that human $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ synthase is composed of an $18\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein that is enzymically active as a homodimer and contains a phosphorylation site [36, 37, 49]. In our photoaffinity labeling studies no $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -binding protein with a molecular mass of $18\mathrm{{kDa}}$ was observed. It remains to be elucidated whether $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ synthase in the murine mastocytoma cell line is associated with a $35\mathrm{-kDa}$ protein which could not be separated into subunits. The $190\mathrm{-k\bar{D}a}$ protein labeled by $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ was found to be a glycoprotein with a mean isoelectric point at $\mathsf{p H}6.3$ (Fig. 7). The relatively broad pI range from $\mathrm{\bar{pH}}5.8$ to $\mathtt{p H6.7}$ is in line with an expected microheterogeneity of the oligosaccharide residues. The characteristics of the ATP-dependent transport of $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ ，especially the susceptibility to cyclosporins [25], suggest that the transport protein is a member of the ATP-binding cassette transporter superfamily [50] and is possibly related to the structure of mdr gene products such as P-glycoprotein. The monoclonal anti-P-glycoprotein antibody C219 did not recognize the $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ -labeled 190-kDa protein in our experiments. In a number of tumor cell lines, resistance to cytotoxic drugs such as colchicine, doxorubicin, and vincristine is related to the presence of mdr1 P-glycoprotein in the cell membrane [18]. Recently, a HL60 cell line selected for resistance to doxorubicin and defective in the cellular accumulation of the drug was shown not to overexpress mdr1 and not to contain detectable amounts of P-glycoprotein [51, 52]. Rather, these cells overexpress the multidrug resistance-related protein (MRP or p-190) which has been identified as a component of a non-P-glycoprotein-mediated multidrug resistance [53]. A cDNA for the MRP has been cloned and, based on the deduced sequence, encodes a mem ber of the superfamily of ATP-dependent transporters [53]. The protein encoded by this cDNA was shown to be a 190-kDa membrane-bound glycoprotein of unknown function. Analysis of subcellular fractions indicated that this protein is present in the endoplasmic reticulum and in the plasma membrane [54]. Interestingly the resistance of MRP-expressing human lung cancer cells could be partly reversed by CsA and PSC 833, with PSC 833 as the less potent resistance modifier [24]. This is in line with the different relative potency of CsA and PSC 833 in our transport inhibition studies (Fig.3). The relation between the 190-kDa $\mathrm{LTC}_{4}$ 1 binding glycoprotein described in this study (Figs $4{-}7)$ ）and the 190-kDa MRP membrane glycoprotein of unknown function is currently being established in our laboratories.
